<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.15.2: The Vertex Form</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02009</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.15.2: The Vertex Form</md:title>
    <md:uuid>daf74cc9-936d-4391-bdd0-23f06c8bed5c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>Here are two sets of equations for quadratic functions you saw earlier. In each set, the expressions that define the output are equivalent. In other words, all the equations in Set 1 define the same function. The same is true for Set 2: all the equations define the same function but they are just written in different forms.</para>
<para><emphasis>Set 1:</emphasis></para>
<para>\(f(x)=x^2+4x\)</para>
<para>\(g(x)=x(x+4)\)</para>
<para>\(h(x)=(x+2)^2−4\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Set 2:</emphasis></para>
<para>\(p(x)=-x^2+6x−5\)</para>
<para>\(q(x)=(5−x)(x−1)\)</para>
<para>\(r(x)=-1(x−3)^2+4\)</para>
<para>The expression that defines \(h\) is written in <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">vertex form</term>. We can show that it is equivalent to the expression defining \(f\) by expanding and simplifying the expression:</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}(x+2)^2-4&amp;=&amp;(x+2)(x+2)-4\\&amp;=&amp;x^2+2x+2x+4-4\\&amp;=&amp;x^2+4x,\;\mathrm{which}\;\mathrm{is}\;f(x)\end{array}\) </para>
<para>If this simplified expression is factored, then we arrive at the expression that defines function \(g\): \(x^2+4x=x(x+4)\). The expression that defines \(g\) is written in <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">factored form</term>. The simplified expression that defines function \(f\) is written in <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">standard form</term>.</para>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Show that the expressions defining \(r\) and \(p\) are equivalent.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>s</emphasis><emphasis>olution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para>Expanding and simplifying function \(r\), \(-1(x−3)^2+4 gives -1(x^2−6x+9)+4\), which equals \(-x^2+6x−9+4\) or \(-x^2+6x−5\).</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<para>For questions 2 – 3, examine the graphs representing the quadratic functions.</para>
