<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Why do you think expressions such as those defining \(h\) and \(r\) are said to be written in vertex form?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The numbers in vertex form seem to give the coordinates of the vertex of the graph. When a positive number is added to the \(x\) in the parentheses, the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex seems to be negative—which is the opposite of that number. When a number is subtracted from \(x\), the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex seems to be positive—which is the opposite of what it looks like.</para>
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Interaction End -->
<div class="os-raise-student-reflection">
  <para class="os-raise-student-reflection-title">Why Should I Care?</para>
  <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/668a0db00bbc24bbceaecf0a660d2ecd86729414"/></media></figure><para>When you launch an object, it does not travel in a straight line. Instead, it follows a curved path called a parabola. This means you have to account for distance, height, and angle if you want to hit your target. You can use quadratic equations to find these measurements. </para>
  <para>The next time you throw a ball, think about the parabolic path that it follows.</para>
  
</div></section>
